00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Now listen to part of a classroom lecture on geographical and related characteristics of Greece and answer questions 31 to 40.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
You have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

3
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Now listen to the lecture and answer questions 31 to 40.

4
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Good morning students! Today we're going to continue our discussion of European countries.

5
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
As you know we've already covered the countries from Northern and Central Europe as well as most of the ones that lie along the Mediterranean.

6
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Today I'm going to talk about Greece.

7
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:53,000
As you know, it is an ancient birthplace of one of the most predominant civilizations to exercise early influence on much of the Western world.

8
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Now, when you think of Greece, you think of its antiquity and ancient civilization.

9
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
You think of Alexander the Great, Plato, Aristotle, Socrates and all the great philosophers.

10
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,000
You think of the Trojan War, the ancient Greek gods and everything in between.

11
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Out of all the European countries we have discussed, Greece is perhaps the richest country in terms of culture and contributions to our modern society.

12
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
More so than the Romans, for the Romans took much of what was Greek and repainted in a Roman fashion.

13
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Greece has been known since ancient times and is located in Southern Europe, specifically Southeastern Europe.

14
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Athens is the capital of this country and it is located in the southwest of the mainland.

15
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
It is also, of course, one of Greece's largest cities.

16
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Greece is situated at the intersection of three continents, Europe, Western Asia and Africa.

17
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:14,000
It is bordered by Albania in the northwest, Macedonia and Bulgaria to the north and Turkey to the northeast.

18
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The Aegean Sea surrounds the east of the mainland, the Ionian Sea is to the west, the Mediterranean Sea is to the south

19
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
and the Adriatic lies in the northwest.

20
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Greece has the largest coastline of all the countries of Europe and is the 11th longest in the world.

21
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Because of its large number of islands, Greece contains a mountainous peninsular mainland with a land boundary of 1168 km.

22
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Greece has many islands totaling around 6000. There are 230 that are inhabited.

23
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:01,000
The climate of Greece is classified into three types, Mediterranean, Alpine and temperate.

24
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:09,000
The temperatures in the Mediterranean rarely reach extremes and at times there is sparse snowfall in some areas.

25
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:17,000
The temperature climate is mainly marked by cold, damp winters and hot, dry summers.

26
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
One of the most prominent geographical features of Greece is the mountain range.

27
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Greece is among a small number of countries that feature a large number of mountains within their territories.

28
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Mount Olympus is the highest peak and has been believed to be the abode of the 12 Olympian gods of ancient Greece.

29
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:48,000
It is the fourth tallest peak in Europe and rises approximately 3000 m above sea level.

30
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:57,000
The Pindus mountain range runs from the northwest to southeast and is further extended to the Peloponnese range.

31
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:05,000
The Pindus range also runs underwater and crosses the Aegean Sea to form the Aegean Islands.

32
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
The largest of these islands, the island of Crete, is controlled by Greece.

33
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Greece also has a large number of islands and islets that lie in the Aegean Sea.

34
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:27,000
The largest island, Crete, extends 250 km from east to west and is 55 km in width at its widest point.

35
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:34,000
It is approximately 150 km south of mainland Greece.

36
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:43,000
The Aegean Islands are a different cluster of seven different islands, six of which lie off the western coast.

37
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
The seventh lies off the southern tip of the Peloponnesian Mountains.

38
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:56,000
The Aegean Islands fill the Aegean Sea in an area that spans between mainland Greece to the west and north,

39
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Anatolia to the east and Crete to the south.

40
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
While much of the climate of Greece is predominantly Mediterranean,

41
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:12,000
the Pindus mountain range divides the country so that the climate is much wetter in the west.

42
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:18,000
It is drier and windier in the east, particularly during the summers.

43
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:25,000
The northern areas have a transitional climate, while other areas have an alpine climate.

44
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:33,000
The average annual temperature varies between 10 to 19.7 degrees Celsius.

45
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:43,000
The winds in Greece are colloquially known as the Meltimi, but they are better known by the name Etzysians.

46
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
These are prominent in the Greek mainland from the mid-summers to early winter.

47
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:57,000
They keep the temperatures on the islands of the Aegean lower than those in the Ionian Sea.

48
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Please be sure to check your answers.

49
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
That is the end of the listening test.
